Z||000005||mrfiero||07-10-2001||09:12 PM||||A weak fuel pump will cause all sorts of weird things (as you found out).  I hope they used a genuine Delco pump.......some aftermarket ones don't hold up.  <P>I just had to replace mine......it was replaced by a previous owner about 2 years ago and it died.  It was the culprit to my dying problems when the enine got hot.<P>At any rate, I'm glad you got it fixed and it helped your Fiero out.  BTW......$320 for a shop to replace a pump is pretty good (I'm assuming it included  the price of the pump).<P>||||reg||1
Z||000006||Monkeyman||07-10-2001||09:37 PM||||I can only assume that they used a GM part as it was done at a GM dealership.  And, yes.  That price included the pump.  I figured that I got a reasonable deal since I take all of my business to them (and my wifes' POS, too), but it's still a ton of money for me.  I generally get the best deal they can make.  I've recommended the shop to a few folks, too.  For anyone interested, the name of the dealership is Dorais Chevrolet in Wabash, IN.  If you happen to be in the area and your Fiero (or any other car for that matter) breaks down, call them up and ask for either Karla or Tim in service.  Tell them I sent ya.<P>BTW--I don't remember who it was that I sent to Dorais from Peru, but if you're here on the forum, email me.  Tim said that you have a beautiful Fiero.  I'd like to see it.<P>------------------<BR>'Consciousness:  That annoying time between naps.'<P>MM||||reg||1
Z||000007||mrfiero||07-10-2001||10:20 PM||||Ha!  I would hope a GM dealer would use Delco parts (but you never know).  I believe a Delco pump retails for over $100, so you really did get a deal for $320.  Most places around here (Denver) charge at least $500 (ouch!).<P>I usually charge around $300 myself (between $3-$400) to do a fuel pump......mainly due to the "pain in the butt" factor.<P>Take it easy.  ||||reg||10
Z||000008||fiero56||07-10-2001||10:29 PM||||I replaced the fuel pump in my '86 SE 2.8 last year. I bought a GM unit from Autozone, and it was something like $89, plus another $7 for the strainer and related pieces.<P>It was a pretty easy afternoon job, if I ever have to do it again, and if the tank isn't full, its an easy 2 hr job. With a full tank, it would probably take longer, assuming the tank is 92lbs heavier than empty.||||reg||1
Z||000009||Tigger||07-10-2001||10:32 PM||||I'm looking at a brand new ACDelco fuel pump in the box for my '84.  <P>I bought it because I thought the pump had died, but instead a twistee connector on a power line to the pump came apart.  Still don't know what the previous owner was thinking.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif">  Someday when I get bored or funny things start to happen, I'll drop the tank and replace it.<P>Anyhoo, I think it cost me about $50-60 for the pump and pickup screen.<P>ACDelco El. Fuel Pump<BR>P/N 25166717||||reg||1
Z||000010||Monkeyman||07-11-2001||04:54 AM||||My tank was within 21 miles of being full so I'm sure that had an effect on the bill.  I checked and it was AC/Delco.  I hate paying the extra for "Genuine GM Quality", but at least I know that there's a better chance of it being good.  It'll feel good to go out this morning to start her up and not have that 10min wait-play with the throttle-hope it makes it to the corner before dying-no power feeling.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>------------------<BR>'Consciousness:  That annoying time between naps.'<P>MM||||reg||1
Z||000011||BBBAD GT||07-11-2001||09:18 AM||||I just finished swapping out the fuel pump in the V8 Fiero.  It had the original 84 pump, and that filter sock was an ugly dark brown.  Anyway, a big pain in the butt getting that tank down and up, but that didn't compare to getting those freakin rubber lines off.  <BR>BTW, my AC Delco pump was only $40 at autozone with an extra $5 for a new sock.  <P>------------------<BR>        <IMG SRC="http://scribers.midwest.net/bbbad50/V8Fiero/V8sig.jpg">        <P><A HREF="http://home.att.net/~nomercy5.0/V8Specs.htm" TARGET=_blank>BBBAD V8 For Sale</A><P><A HREF="http://scribers.midwest.net/bbbad50/" TARGET=_blank>BBBAD 5.0</A>||||reg||1
Z||000012||Alex4mula||07-11-2001||11:05 AM||||I second that of the A/C Delco pump. ONLY buy that one. I once put an aftermarket POS and after 20K miles I had to changed again. At least I learned how to do it myslef...<P>------------------<BR>Alex4mula<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.zing.com/picture/p69fbfe3e99ec5a0b81c10afcc578ff95/fe6d5c42.jpg.orig.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000013||mblackwell||07-11-2001||01:04 PM||||Mine died at around 105k miles.  Before it died I had a lot of trouble with cold starts.  My uncle and I installed it ourselves in my driveway.  We used a $40 one that came in a box labled "Bomba De Gasalina".  It runs A LOT better now than it did the few months before it died.  Big differance.||||reg||1
Z||000014||Black88GT||07-11-2001||01:39 PM||||Just as a note, for the high performance guru's, the '96 Corvette LT4 fuel pump will work in Fiero's, its good for about 400 Hp. <P>I got one from GMpartsdirect.com for 80 bucks shipped. ||||reg||12
Z||000015||stsmithgt||07-11-2001||01:49 PM||||I had one replaced last year with the GM part especially because the one that was pulled out was aftermarket from the previous original owner.  It cost about 300.00 also.  The bad one would make my idle and gas gauge freak out from all the power the old pump was draining off the system(so I was told).  It's wierd though, it would run wide open just fine if I could get the RPM's up, but that was tough in town.  On the freeway, it was Zoooom! coast, coast ,coast, sputter, Zoooom! At least I made it home.||||reg||1
